From 9d1bbb4f76e51aa29452735d97f70d335b6b1271 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 01:45:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] devices: virtio-nvgpu as a vhost-user device type

`--vhost-user type=nvgpu,socket=PATH` connects a virtio-nvgpu backend
(NVIDIA driver calls forwarded to the host; virtio device ID 45, not one
the virtio specification assigns). Only the vhost-user frontend exists:
the backend walks the queues, and crosvm walks none.

What the type needs of the generic frontend:
- PCI class 0xff0000 (unassigned). The device forwards another GPU's
  driver calls and drives no display, and a display-class function is
  what guest userspace takes for a GPU of its own: Chromium's GPU
  blocklist, for one, saw a second GPU.
- Indirect descriptors (VIRTIO_RING_F_INDIRECT_DESC), which the generic
  feature mask drops. They let one ring slot carry a request of up to
  4 MiB; without them driver and backend keep to 256 KiB. A ring feature
  costs this process nothing: it never reads a descriptor.
- Region 1 when the backend reports more than one shared memory region.
  Region 1 is the window every guest maps GPU memory through; region 2,
  the UVM aperture, only CUDA uses, and the PCI transport publishes one
  region per device. With region 2 left out the guest driver finds no
  aperture and offers no compute.
- Only SHMEM_MAP and SHMEM_UNMAP: GPU_MAP and EXTERNAL_MAP are refused
  (EPERM), so a backend that is taken over cannot reach the gralloc or
  external-pointer paths.

